      <h1>Battery Failure Modes</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Technology Reference · Last reviewed 2026-09-15</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Lead-acid and lithium batteries fail through different mechanisms, but the
        pattern is the same — <strong>a specific abuse condition produces a specific chemical or physical
        degradation</strong>. Lead-acid fails mainly by sulfation, grid corrosion, water loss and stratification;
        lithium by SEI growth, lithium plating (and its dendrites) and, in the worst case, thermal runaway.
      </div>

      <h2>Lead-acid failure modes</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>What happens</th><th>Main cause</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Sulfation</strong></td><td>Lead sulfate crystals harden and stop converting back to active material</td><td>Chronic undercharging / stored discharged</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Grid corrosion</strong></td><td>The positive lead grid corrodes, raising resistance and shedding material</td><td>Overcharging, high temperature, age</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Water loss / dry-out</strong></td><td>Overcharge electrolyzes water into gas; electrolyte is lost (irrecoverable in sealed/AGM)</td><td>Overcharging</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Acid stratification</strong></td><td>Acid concentrates at the bottom of the cell, so plates wear unevenly</td><td>Partial charging, no gassing/mixing</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Internal short</strong></td><td>Separator failure or plate shedding lets plates touch</td><td>Vibration, deep cycling, age</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Lithium failure modes</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>What happens</th><th>Main cause</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>SEI growth</strong></td><td>The passivating layer thickens, consuming lithium and raising impedance</td><td>Time, heat, high state of charge</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Lithium plating</strong></td><td>Metallic lithium deposits on the anode instead of intercalating (irreversible)</td><td>Fast charge, cold temperature</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Dendrite short</strong></td><td>Plated lithium grows needle-like and can pierce the separator</td><td>Repeated plating</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Over-discharge</strong></td><td>Cell voltage falls too low; copper can dissolve and re-deposit as a short</td><td>Deep discharge below the cut-off</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Thermal runaway</strong></td><td>An exothermic chain reaction self-heats the cell to fire</td><td>Internal short, overcharge, external heat</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>The common thread</h2>
      <p>
        Both chemistries are governed by the same truth: <strong>each failure mode has a specific trigger</strong>, and
        most triggers are operating conditions, not bad luck. Sulfation is undercharging; water loss is overcharging;
        plating is fast-and-cold charging. This is why the fix is usually a habit, not a hardware change.
      </p>

      <h2>What This Means</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>My position:</strong> The most useful way to read a failure-mode table is not to memorize it, but to
        see that almost every mode is a <em>charging</em> problem in disguise.</p>
        <p><strong>What drives this:</strong> Undercharge sulfates, overcharge gasses and corrodes, fast-cold charge plates.
        Get the charging right and you have already avoided the majority of early battery deaths.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is my analysis, not a verified fact or purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

